KG42.register("rules-e80149ee47ca7f3b41713d27",{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R010","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Inter-area clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"Submitted neighboring energy requests provide inputs to the update of μG-i's intermicrogrid selling price. Scope: Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During each selling-price update using requests collected from neighboring microgrids. Exchange of the microgrids' demand bids precedes the regulation phase that adjusts intermicrogrid energy prices. Scope: The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. Condition: Within each algorithm iteration. The locally updated offered energy quantity ε_i^{(s)}[k] is an input to μG-i's next selling-price update. Scope: Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. Condition: During each algorithm iteration after the local offered quantity has been updated. The intermicrogrid market-clearing process adjusts energy prices according to the law of demand until global demand matches offer. Scope: The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During iterative clearing, until global energy demand matches the energy offer. The selling price must increase; the source states λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]. Scope: The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. Condition: The energy offered by μG-i is less than the total energy demanded from it by neighboring microgrids. μG-i's selling unit price equals its marginal generation cost. Each supplying μG-j's unit price equals μG-i's selling price minus the marginal transportation cost of the purchased energy. Scope: Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. Condition: At the optimal solution λ^*, with j∈S_* for the supplying microgrids. μG-4 sells at a unit price given by its marginal generation cost and does not seek extra gains. Scope: μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example. Condition: After convergence, μG-4 operates in Case 4 (generates and sells), under the total-system-cost objective and the prohibition on cheating.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.inter_area under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply","clearing.inter_area","pricing.inter_area"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R010-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"pricing.inter_area","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R010-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g20-e020","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g23-e023"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R010-E002","source":"bidding.demand","target":"pricing.inter_area","type":"precedes","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R010-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g22-e022"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R010-E003","source":"bidding.supply","target":"pricing.inter_area","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R010-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R010-E004","source":"clearing.inter_area","target":"pricing.inter_area","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R010-C004"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g24-e024"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. | Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. | Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. | The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. | The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. | The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. | μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e06","v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g20-e020","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g23-e023","v141-b-g24-e024","v141-b-g38-e038"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X001","symbol":"\\lambda_i[k]","meaning":"μG-i's inter-microgrid selling price at iteration k; the source interprets the Lagrange multiplier λ_i as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell to neighbors.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued price signal; sign not explicitly constrained in the supplied text","unit":"USD/MWh (unit price per energy unit)","indices":["i","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g23-e023","v141-b-g24-e024"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X002","symbol":"\\varepsilon_i^{(s)}[k]","meaning":"Energy offered for sale by μG-i at iteration k, introduced as the new local selling quantity in the reformulated clearing problem.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\varepsilon_i^{(s)}[k] \\ge 0","unit":"MWh","indices":["i","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g22-e022"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X003","symbol":"E_i^{(s)}[k]","meaning":"Offered-energy vector of μG-i built from the neighbor-specific quantities E_{i,1}[k],...,E_{i,M}[k] and used in the price-update mismatch term.","kind":"operational","domain":"vector with nonnegative entries E_{i,j}[k] \\ge 0","unit":"MWh","indices":["i","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g22-e022"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X004","symbol":"\\lambda_i^*","meaning":"Optimal inter-microgrid selling price of μG-i at the dual optimum λ^*; interpreted as the unitary selling price.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued optimal price signal; sign not explicitly constrained in the supplied text","unit":"USD/MWh (unit price per energy unit)","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g38-e038"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X005","symbol":"\\lambda_j^*","meaning":"At μG-i, the optimal unit buying price from supplying μG-j; interpreted as the corresponding supplier's unit price at the optimum.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued optimal price signal; sign not explicitly constrained in the supplied text","unit":"USD/MWh (unit price per energy unit)","indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g38-e038"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X006","symbol":"E_i^{(g)}","meaning":"Energy generated by μG-i at the optimum, entering the marginal generation-cost price relation.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative generated energy, E_i^{(g)} \\ge 0, from the local-balance inequality in the supplied model","unit":"MWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g02-e023"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X007","symbol":"E_{j,i}","meaning":"Energy transferred from μG-j to μG-i at the optimum, i.e. the quantity bought by μG-i from supplier μG-j in the optimal price relation.","kind":"operational","domain":"E_{j,i} \\ge 0","unit":"MWh","indices":["j","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g02-e023"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-X008","symbol":"\\varepsilon_s^{(4)}","meaning":"Energy sold by μG-4 after convergence in the four-microgrid numerical example.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative sold energy in the Case 4 example","unit":"MWh","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e02"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T001","symbol":"\\alpha[k]","meaning":"Positive step factor used in the iterative price update.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g20-e020","v141-b-g22-e022"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T002","symbol":"A","meaning":"Directed adjacency matrix [a_{i,j}]_{M×M} with a_{i,j}=1 if a connection from μG-i to μG-j exists and 0 otherwise, a_{i,i}=0.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"[a_{i,j}]_{M×M}, a_{i,j}∈{0,1}, a_{i,i}=0"},"unit":null,"indices":["i","j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g02-e010"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T003","symbol":"e_i","meaning":"The i-th column of the M×M identity matrix, used to select the i-th component in the price-update expression.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"i-th column of M×M identity matrix"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g10-e010","v141-b-g22-e022"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T004","symbol":"C_i'(\\cdot)","meaning":"Marginal generation-cost function of μG-i, i.e. the first derivative of the generation cost C_i(·).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"C_i'(·)"},"unit":"USD/MWh (marginal cost per energy unit)","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g27-e027","v141-b-g38-e038","v141-b-g02-e023"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T005","symbol":"\\gamma'(\\cdot)","meaning":"Marginal transportation-cost function, i.e. the first derivative of the transfer cost γ(·).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"γ'(·)"},"unit":"USD/MWh (marginal cost per energy unit)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g27-e027","v141-b-g38-e038"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T006","symbol":"S_*","meaning":"Set of supplying microgrids at the optimal Case 6 solution; the source defines S_* = {j : λ_j \u003c λ_i - γ'(0)}.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"{j : λ_j \u003c λ_i - γ'(0)} in Case 6"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g35-e035","v141-b-g02-e023"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T007","symbol":"E_i^{(c)}","meaning":"Local energy consumption (load) of μG-i, treated as a fixed input parameter.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"E_i^{(c)}"},"unit":"MWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g37-e037","v141-b-g03-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-T008","symbol":"C'(\\cdot)","meaning":"Common marginal generation-cost function in the four-microgrid numerical example, where C_i(·)=C(·) for i=1,2,3,4.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"C'(·)"},"unit":"USD/MWh (marginal cost per energy unit)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e02"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Submitted neighboring energy requests provide inputs to the update of μG-i's intermicrogrid selling price. Scope: Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During each selling-price update using requests collected from neighboring microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g20-e020","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g23-e023"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Exchange of the microgrids' demand bids precedes the regulation phase that adjusts intermicrogrid energy prices. Scope: The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. Condition: Within each algorithm iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g22-e022"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The locally updated offered energy quantity ε_i^{(s)}[k] is an input to μG-i's next selling-price update. Scope: Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. Condition: During each algorithm iteration after the local offered quantity has been updated.","expression":"\\lambda_i[k+1] = \\lambda_i[k] + \\alpha[k]\\left(e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - \\varepsilon_i^{(s)}[k]\\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-X001","v141-c-R010-X002","v141-c-R010-X003"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-T001","v141-c-R010-T002","v141-c-R010-T003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024","v141-b-g19-e019"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The intermicrogrid market-clearing process adjusts energy prices according to the law of demand until global demand matches offer. Scope: The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During iterative clearing, until global energy demand matches the energy offer.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area","pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g24-e024"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The selling price must increase; the source states λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]. Scope: The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. Condition: The energy offered by μG-i is less than the total energy demanded from it by neighboring microgrids.","expression":"\\varepsilon_i^{(s)}[k] \u003c e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] \\implies \\lambda_i[k+1] \\ge \\lambda_i[k]","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-X001","v141-c-R010-X002","v141-c-R010-X003"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-T002","v141-c-R010-T003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g24-e024"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"μG-i's selling unit price equals its marginal generation cost. Each supplying μG-j's unit price equals μG-i's selling price minus the marginal transportation cost of the purchased energy. Scope: Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. Condition: At the optimal solution λ^*, with j∈S_* for the supplying microgrids.","expression":"\\lambda_i^* = C_i'(E_i^{(g)}), \\quad \\lambda_j^* = \\lambda_i^* - \\gamma'(E_{j,i}) \\quad \\forall j \\in S_*","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-X004","v141-c-R010-X005","v141-c-R010-X006","v141-c-R010-X007"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-T004","v141-c-R010-T005","v141-c-R010-T006"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g38-e038","v141-b-g35-e035"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"μG-4 sells at a unit price given by its marginal generation cost and does not seek extra gains. Scope: μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example. Condition: After convergence, μG-4 operates in Case 4 (generates and sells), under the total-system-cost objective and the prohibition on cheating.","expression":"\\lambda_4^* = C'\\left(E_4^{(c)} + \\varepsilon_s^{(4)}\\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-X004","v141-c-R010-X008"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-T007","v141-c-R010-T008"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e06","v141-b-g03-e02"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e023","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-C. Interpretation and Summary","anchor_text":"diture Ci (λ ) in (7), where λ stands for the optimal point of (5). For the sake of brevity, we prov","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Next, by means of the results of Case 6, one has \\lambda^*_i = C'_i(E^{(g)}_i) and \\lambda^*_j = \\lambda^*_i - \\gamma'(E_{j,i}) for all j \\in S_*."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"propose an algorithm based on dual decomposition that itera- tively solves the problem in a distribu","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Interestingly, each iteration of the resulting algorithm has a straightforward interpretation in economical terms once the new operational variables we introduce are given the meaning of energy prices. First, each microgrid locally computes the amounts of energy it must produce, buy, and sell to minimize its local cost according to the current energy prices. Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand. This two-step process iterates until a global agreement is reached about prices and transferred energies."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"gy g To understand why this happens, let us consider μG-4. After μ convergence, μG-4 is deﬁned by Ca","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"After convergence, μG-4 is defined by Case 4 (generates and sells). Then, particularizing (7), the local cost at μG-4 is \\mathcal{C}_4 = C\\big(E_4^{(c)} + \\varepsilon_s^{(4)}\\big) - \\lambda_4\\varepsilon_s^{(4)} \\quad (10) where we used the fact that we are assuming C_i(\\cdot) = C(\\cdot) for i = 1, 2, 3, 4."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"fter μG-4 at low values of E(c)4 . ls). Finally, let us recall that the purpose of the original prob","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Finally, let us recall that the purpose of the original problem (3) is to minimize the total cost of the system and not to maximize local benefits. This, together with the fact that we do not allow μGs to cheat, explains why μG-4 always sells at a unit price given by the marginal cost and does not look for extra gains."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Conclusion","anchor_text":"G 1 keeps local cost functions and local consumption private. More speciﬁcally, each algorithm itera","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"Next, during the market clearing process, energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g20-e020","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"t at t e set o vecto s { i } ca be ead y de ved ow g set {E(b)i }. Note that vector ς = [eTi AE(s)i ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where α[k] is a positive step factor. In addition, recall from (2) that the set of vectors {E_i^{(s)}} can be readily derived knowing set {E_i^{(b)}}. Note that vector ς = [e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k]]_{M×1} is a subgradient of the dual concave function C(λ) in λ = λ[k], i.e., C(λ) ≤ C(λ[k]) + ς^T(λ - λ[k]) ∀λ. Finally, (8) also says that λ_i can be updated at μG-i once vector E_i^{(s)}[k] has been built with inputs E_{i,j}[k] collected from the neighboring μGs."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"μG-i initialize λi[0] repeat μGs exchange {λi[k]} sh μG-i computes ε(s)i [k] and E(b)i [k] by solvin","equation_id":"Algorithm 1","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Algorithm 1 Distributed approach μG-i initialize λ_i[0] repeat μGs exchange {λ_i[k]} μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k] μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k] with energy requests E_{i,j}[k] from neighboring μGs, μG-i builds E_i^{(s)}[k] ← [E_{i,1}[k] ... E_{i,M}[k]]^T μG-i computes λ_i[k+1] ← λ_i[k] + α[k](e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k]) k ← k+1 until convergence condition is verified"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g23-e023","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each Lagrange multiplier λ_i may be understood as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell energy to its neighbors. Then, Lagrangian function (7) can be seen as the “net expenditure” (the opposite of the net income) for μG-i, i.e., each μG pays for producing energy, for buying energy, and for transporting the energy it buys. Conversely, the μG is paid for the energy it sells. By solving problem (6), μG-i is thus maximizing its benefit for some given selling (λ_i[k]) and buying (λ_j[k], j≠i) prices per energy unit."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g38-e038","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-C. Interpretation and Summary","anchor_text":"It is interesting to note that the optimal solution provided in the previous section has a straightf","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Recall that, at μG-i, the Lagrange multipliers can be interpreted as the unitary selling price (λ_i) and the unitary buying prices from the other μGs, that is {λ_j}, with j≠i. Moreover, derivatives C_i'(·) and γ'(·) are the marginal generation cost and the marginal transportation cost, respectively, that are the linear variation on the cost due to an infinitesimal variation of the generated or transported energy, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-B. μG Subproblem","anchor_text":"set {λj}, j̸ =i are ordered in an increasing order, i.e., λmin =λ1 ≤ s a λ2 ≤· · ·≤λi−1 ≤λi+1 ≤· · ·","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Additionally, with some abuse of notation, we fix \\lambda_j = +\\infty when a_{j,i} = 0, i.e., as far as \\mu G-i is concerned, the fact that there is no connection from \\mu G-j to \\mu G-i is equivalent to the assumption that the price of the energy sold by \\mu G-j is too high to be worth buying."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"gy g To understand why this happens, let us consider μG-4. After μ convergence, μG-4 is deﬁned by Ca","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The optimal λ_4 = \\lambda_4^* is given by the marginal cost, i.e., \\lambda_4^* = C'\\big(E_4^{(c)} + \\varepsilon_s^{(4)}\\big). \\quad (11)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Consider a system composed of M interconnected microgrids (μGs) operating in islanded mode. During each scheduling interval, each microgrid μG-i generates E_i^{(g)} units of energy and consumes E_i^{(c)} units of energy. Moreover, μG-i may be allowed to sell energy E_{i,j} to μG-j, j≠i, and to buy energy E_{k,i} from μG-k, k≠i. Then, the energy equilibrium within the μG requires E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g06-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"to sell energy Ei,j to μG j, j̸ i, and to buy energy Ek,i fromrgy μG-k, k̸=i. Then, the energy equil","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the two M-dimensional column vectors, i.e., E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T, E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T (2) gather all the energy quantities bought and sold, respectively, by μG-i. In addition, we have introduced adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}, where element a_{i,j} is equal to 1 if there exists a connection from μG-i to μG-j and 0 otherwise. Note that, generally, A may be nonsymmetric, meaning that at least two μGs are allowed to exchange energy in one direction only. By convention, we fix a_{i,i}=0. Moreover, a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh for all i,j=1,...,M."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g10-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"GREGORATTI AND MATAMOROS: DISTRIBUTED ENERGY TRADING: MULTIPLE-MIC where ei is the ith column of the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where e_i is the ith column of the M×M identity matrix and, with some abuse of notation, we wrote γ(E_i^{(b)})=[γ(E_{1,i}) ... γ(E_{M,i})]^T. In addition, we used (1) to get rid of variables {E_i^{(g)}}. Note that problem (3) considers the μGs as parts of a common system (for instance, they are controlled by the same operator), and the aim is at minimizing the global cost, without focusing on the benefits/losses of each individual μG. We will see later on, however, that the proposed distributed and iterative minimization algorithm opens to a wider sense interpretation, where achieving the global objective implies a cost reduction at every μG."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g15-e015","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"i i i i i at {ε(s)i },{Ei,j} i=1 + eTi AT γ(E(b)i ) i=1 s.t. Ei,j ≥0 ∀i,j ks ey E(c)i + ε(s)i −eTi A","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}) s.t. E_{i,j} ≥ 0 ∀i,j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i, ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i. (4) The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g19-e019","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"2554 IEEE TR point (ε(s)i [k], E(b)i [k]) = (ε(s)i (λ[k]), E(b)i (λ[k])). Then, the B Lagrange multi","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"λ[k+1] = λ[k] + α[k] [ e_1^T A E_1^{(s)}[k] - ε_1^{(s)}[k] ; ⋮ ; e_M^T A E_M^{(s)}[k] - ε_M^{(s)}[k] ] (8)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g27-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-B. μG Subproblem","anchor_text":"set {λj}, j̸ =i are ordered in an increasing order, i.e., λmin =λ1 ≤ s a λ2 ≤· · ·≤λi−1 ≤λi+1 ≤· · ·","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Moreover, we will make use of functions C_i'(·) and γ'(·) (the first derivatives of cost functions C_i(·) and γ(·), respectively) and of their inverse functions, i.e., χ_i(·) and Γ(·), respectively. It is interesting to mention that, in economics, the derivative of a cost function is called the marginal cost (the cost of infinitesimally increasing the argument)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g35-e035","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-B. μG Subproblem","anchor_text":"IPLE-MICROGRID CASE 2555 − the energy it consumes, plus some extra energy for the market, from all t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Case 6 (μG-i sells, buys, and generates): Assume that λ_i > C_i'(0), λ_min \u003c λ_i - γ'(0), and λ_i > C_i'(E_i^{(c)} - ∑_{j∈S_*} Γ(λ_i - λ_j)) where we introduced set S_* = {j : λ_j \u003c λ_i - γ'(0)}. Then, the local generator is activated, but μG-i also buys energy from all the μGs in S_*. After feeding all local loads with E_i^{(c)}, some extra energy is left for selling in the market as follows: E_{j,i} = Γ(λ_i - λ_j) ∀j∈S_*   E_{j,i} = 0 ∀j∉S_* ε_i^{(s)} = E_i^{(g)} + ∑_{j∈S_*} E_{j,i} - E_i^{(c)}   E_i^{(g)} = χ_i(λ_i)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g37-e037","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-C. Interpretation and Summary","anchor_text":"assumptions are necessary for each given case. Furthermore, one can also derive the exact values of ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As a final remark, note that we are assuming a positive load at the μGs, i.e., E_i^{(c)} > 0 ∀i. The case where E_i^{(c)} = 0 may be analogously handled and brings similar results. More specifically, Cases 4, 5, and 6 directly extend because of continuity. Conversely, Cases 2 and 3 disappear and expand the domain of Case 1 to λ_i ≤ C_i'(0) and λ_min ≥ λ_i - γ'(0)."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.inter_area","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g20-e020","quote":"Finally, (8) also says that λ_i can be updated at μG-i once vector E_i^{(s)}[k] has been built with inputs E_{i,j}[k] collected from the neighboring μGs."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","quote":"μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g23-e023","quote":"Each Lagrange multiplier λ_i may be understood as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell energy to its neighbors."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","quote":"Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","quote":"μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k]"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","quote":"μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","quote":"μG-i computes λ_i[k+1] ← λ_i[k] + α[k](e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k])"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","quote":"If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e10","quote":"Next, during the market clearing process, energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","quote":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","quote":"If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e023","quote":"Next, by means of the results of Case 6, one has \\lambda^*_i = C'_i(E^{(g)}_i) and \\lambda^*_j = \\lambda^*_i - \\gamma'(E_{j,i}) for all j \\in S_*."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g38-e038","quote":"Moreover, derivatives C_i'(·) and γ'(·) are the marginal generation cost and the marginal transportation cost, respectively"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","quote":"After convergence, μG-4 is defined by Case 4 (generates and sells)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e06","quote":"Finally, let us recall that the purpose of the original problem (3) is to minimize the total cost of the system and not to maximize local benefits. This, together with the fact that we do not allow μGs to cheat, explains why μG-4 always sells at a unit price given by the marginal cost and does not look for extra gains."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"2be40b71c35544bcbfc6c34af4b58be6709cdea5d5f90fe2ad9cea3395864110","applicability":"formalized","reason":"Supported source equations are present for the iterative selling-price update, the law-of-demand inequality, the optimal Case 6 price relations, and the μG-4 marginal-cost pricing statement. Purely sequential or process-description constraints C001, C002 and C004 are retained as not_formalized. B evidence remains unreviewed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X001","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"λ_i[k] is the pricing.inter_area object updated in C003; edge E003 carries the offered supply quantity into that price update."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X002","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"ε_i^{(s)}[k] is part of the supplied-energy quantity whose mismatch drives the inter-area price update through edge E003."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X003","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"E_i^{(s)}[k] is the offered-energy vector used in the price update and therefore binds to the supply node and its link to pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X004","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E004"],"rationale":"λ_i^* is the optimal inter-area selling price obtained from clearing; edge E004 links clearing.inter_area to pricing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X005","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E004"],"rationale":"λ_j^* is the neighbor-specific optimal buying/supplier price in the same clearing-based pricing relation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X006","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"E_i^{(g)} determines the marginal generation-cost component of the seller's price and is bound to supply and its pricing link."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X007","node_ids":["bidding.demand"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E001"],"rationale":"E_{j,i} is the purchased/requested energy quantity from supplier j; edge E001 links demand-side requests to pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-X008","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"ε_s^{(4)} is μG-4's sold energy in the example and binds to supply feeding the price formula via edge E003."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T001","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"α[k] is the step factor in the selling-price update supported through the supply-to-pricing edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T002","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"A enters the neighbor-aggregation term of the price update and is bound to the pricing node and supply edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T003","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"e_i selects the i-th component of the supply-aggregation vector in the price update."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T004","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"C_i'(·) is the marginal generation cost behind the seller price relation and binds to the supply side feeding pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T005","node_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E004"],"rationale":"γ'(·) is the marginal transportation cost in the optimal inter-area price relation; edge E004 links clearing to pricing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T006","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E004"],"rationale":"S_* selects the suppliers in the optimal Case 6 price relation at the clearing outcome."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T007","node_ids":["bidding.demand"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E001"],"rationale":"E_i^{(c)} is the fixed local load parameter affecting demand-side pricing and binds through the demand-to-pricing edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-T008","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"C'(·) is the common marginal generation-cost function in the μG-4 example and binds to supply-side price formation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-C003","node_ids":["bidding.supply","pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"The formal update uses ε_i^{(s)}[k] from bidding.supply to compute λ_i[k+1] at pricing.inter_area, exactly along edge E003."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-C005","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E001","v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"The formal law-of-demand implication compares supplied energy (edge E003) with demanded energy (edge E001) before asserting the price inequality at pricing.inter_area."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-C006","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E004"],"rationale":"The Case 6 optimal price relations are clearing-determined pricing outcomes, bound through the clearing-to-pricing edge E004."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R010-C007","node_ids":["pricing.inter_area"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R010-E003"],"rationale":"The μG-4 marginal-cost selling-price expression is a pricing outcome fed by μG-4's supplied energy through edge E003."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
